      "title": "Shape distance, TPS warp, generalized Hough",
      "properties": {},
      "widgets_values": [
        "OpenCV's 'shape' module and the generalized Hough transform - all of them cv2 classes, so each comes as a curated node rather than a raw cv2.* wrapper.\n\nIMPORTANT if you ever call these classes yourself: they MUST come from their create* factory. Constructing cv2.ShapeContextDistanceExtractor() directly yields the abstract base and every call on it dies with 'Unknown C++ exception'.\n\nRow 1 - 'CV Shape Distance' scores how different two contours are. Shape Context builds a log-polar histogram around each sample point and solves the correspondence, so it tolerates bending and scale; Hausdorff is the (rank-filtered) worst-case nearest-point distance in PIXELS, cheap but only meaningful on aligned shapes. Both resample the contours to a common length first, which is what lets contours of different lengths be compared at all.\n\nRow 2 - 'CV Thin Plate Spline Warp' bends an image so that every 'from' point lands exactly on its 'to' point while minimising bending energy. Where a homography has 8 parameters and keeps lines straight, a TPS has as many as you give it control points. regularization 0 interpolates exactly (see the ~0 residual); raising it trades exactness for a gentler warp.\n\nRow 3 - 'CV Generalized Hough' finds an arbitrary SHAPE from a template image by letting every scene EDGE vote for the shape's reference point. Unlike cv2_matchTemplate, which correlates raw pixels, it only needs the outline - so partial occlusion and colour or lighting changes do not matter. Ballard searches TRANSLATION only, so the template must appear at the same scale and rotation (here it is a crop of the scene); switch to Guil - keeping the scale and angle ranges TIGHT - when it does not.\n\nTry: swap the two contour indices; raise the TPS regularization and watch the residual grow; lower votes_threshold if the Hough finds nothing."
      ],
      "color": "#432",
      "bgcolor": "#653"
    },
